'''''Ceratiola''''' is a [[genus]] of [[flowering plant]]s with a single species, '''''Ceratiola ericoides''''', a [[shrub]] endemic to the [[Southeastern United States]].

==Names==
''C. ericoides'' is commonly known as '''Florida rosemary''',{{Sfn|Johnson|1982|p=170}} '''sand heath''',<ref>{{PLANTS|id=CEER3|taxon=Ceratiola ericoides|accessdate=24 February 2024}}</ref> '''scrub rosemary''',<ref>{{Cite web |date=May 16, 2017 |title=Scrub rosemary (Ceratiola ericoides) |url=https://arboretum.ucf.edu/virtual/scrub-rosemary/ |access-date=24 February 2024 |website=The Virtual UCF (University of Central Florida) Arboretum}}</ref> or '''sandhill rosemary'''.<ref>{{Cite web |date=December 11, 2018 |title=December Native Plant of the Month: Sandhill rosemary |url=https://botgarden.uga.edu/december-native-plant/ |access-date=25 February 2024 |website=State Botanical Garden of Georgia}}</ref>

== Description ==
Florida rosemary can grow to {{Convert|6|ft|order=flip}} tall. The growth form is a rounded shrub with dense branching. Dark green leaves are needle-like, eight to twelve mm long and one mm wide, and smell like the herb [[rosemary]]. The yellow to brown flowers are small. The green or yellow fruit is {{Convert|3|mm|frac=8}} in diameter, and is a juicy [[drupe]] containing two seeds.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Miller |first1=Debbie |last2=Thetford |first2=Mark |date=October 4, 2018 |title=Florida rosemany, ''Ceratiola ericoides'' |url=https://edis.ifas.ufl.edu/publication/SG164 |access-date=27 February 2024 |website=IFAS Extension}}</ref>

=== Reproduction ===
[[File:Ceratiola ericoides 108130750.jpg|thumb|]]
The Florida rosemary is [[dioecious]] and [[wind pollinated]]. Flowers are  in the [[Leaf#Morphology|leaf axils]] on the previous years growth.{{Sfn|Gibson|Menges|1994|p=338}} Flowers bloom on leaf axils of the previous year's growth from September through October. Fruits ripen in January through April. Flowers and fruit may remain on the plant year-round.{{Sfn|Schmidt|2007|loc=Study species}} Plants do not produce seed until they are 10 to 15 years old. Seed production per plant increases until age 20 to 30 years. Seed production begins to fall off in plants more than 35 years old.{{Sfn|Johnson|1982|pp=171–172}}{{Efn|The age of Florida rosemary plants may be estimated by counting branching nodes on main stems, as branching normally occurs once a year. Node count correlates closely to [[Dendrochronology|ring count]] in the base stem. On older plants, older branches may die, or droop down to the ground, establishing roots at the contact point, so that nodes on the new growth are not matched by growth rings in the original stem, making it difficult to determine the age of such plants.{{Sfn|Johnson|1982|pp=170–172}}}}

Florida rosemary plants release a chemical called ceratiolin into the soil, which breaks down into [[Phenylpropanoic acid|hydrocinnamic acid]], which in turn inhibits the growth of other plants, and of rosemary seeds (a process called [[allelopathy]]). Decomposition by [[microbiota]], fire, and other disturbances to the soil break down the hydrocinnamic acid, allowing other plants to grow, and rosemary seeds to sprout.{{Sfn|Richardson|Hall|2004|p=12}}

==Taxonomy==
''Ceratiola ericoides'' is the [[monotypic taxon|sole species]] in the genus ''Ceratiola''.<ref name="APA_Ceratiola">{{cite web |title=Ceratiola ericoides |url=http://floraofalabama.org/Plant.aspx?id=1725# |accessdate=25 February 2024 |website=Alabama Plant Atlas}}</ref> It was formerly included in the [[Family (taxonomy)|plant family]] Empetraceae, which since 2002 has been reclassified as the [[Tribe (biology)|tribe]] Empetreae in the [[Ericoideae]] subfamily of the [[Ericaceae]] family.{{Sfn|Johnson|1982|p=170}}{{Sfn|Kron|Judd|Stevens|Crayn|2002|pp=336-337}}

== Distribution and habitat ==
The species is native to subtropical scrub and dry sandy habitats on the [[Atlantic Plain#Coastal Plain|Atlantic Coastal Plain]] in the southeastern United States, including [[Georgia (U.S. state)|Georgia]] and [[South Carolina]], throughout [[Florida]], and in coastal counties of [[Alabama]] and [[Mississippi]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Species: Ceratiola ericoides |url=https://www.fs.usda.gov/database/feis/plants/shrub/cereri/all.html |access-date=25 February 2024 |website=Fire Effects Information System |publisher=United States Department of Agriculture Forest Service}}</ref> It commonly occurs in [[Florida scrub]]s together with [[sand pine]] and [[Scrub oak#In the Southeastern United States|evergreen scrub oaks]].{{Sfn|Myers|1990|p=151}}

== Ecology ==
[[File:CeratiolaEicoides8.jpg|thumb|]]
The fruit and seeds are consumed primarily by the [[Pogonomyrmex badius|Florida harvester ant]], the [[oldfield mouse]], the [[eastern towhee]], and the [[Florida scrub jay]]. Seeds eaten by ants and mice are destroyed, while those in fruits eaten by birds pass through the birds' digestive tracts unharmed. Seeds in soil lose viability in less than 10 years.{{Sfn|Johnson|1982|p=172}}

Florida scrub communities typically experience fires at 15 to 100 year intervals,{{Sfn|Menges|Hawkes|1998|pp=936–937}} which kill all plants, including rosemary.{{Sfn|Myers|1990|pp=167–168}} If a scrub patch burns less than ten years after a previous fire, there will not be seeds available in the soil to sprout into new rosemary plants. Johnson suggests that the falloff in seed production after a plant passes 35 years in age indicates that fires usually occur no more than 40 years after a previous fire, so that there is no selective pressure to continue producing large seed crops past 40 years.{{Sfn|Johnson|1982|p=173}}

